{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LNvfbTMnNokUPJMo5gJ1cM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Research Patterns\n3\tdescription: Experimental consensus on variance sources, decomposition methods, and gaps in LLM evaluation reproducibility (10 papers, 2024-2026)\n4\ttype: research\n5\tsource_papers: 10\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Variance Source Taxonomy\n9\t\n10\t**Infrastructure variance** (4/10; high confidence):\n11\t- Numerical precision: BF16→9% accuracy std; FP32→~0% (Yuan 2025). Reasoning models most affected.\n12\t- Batch-size dependence: 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-invariance failure in RMSNorm/GEMM/attention (ThinkingMachines 2025).\n13\t- Background randomness at T=0: GPT~0.075, Gemini~0.065, Claude~0 effective temperature (Messina TMLR 2026).\n14\t\n15\t**Methodological variance** (6/10; high confidence):\n16\t- Judge choice dominates safety eval (43.8% variance); prompt~50% in open models, ~25% commercial (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026).\n17\t- Much apparent prompt sensitivity is evaluation artifact — LLM-judge raises rank correlation 0.30→0.95 (Hua EMNLP 2025).\n18\t- Standard CIs omit 40-60% uncertainty; naive 95% CIs→79% coverage at scale (Mesting 2026).\n19\t\n20\t## Decomposition Frameworks\n21\t\n22\t| Framework | Paper | Method |\n23\t|-----------|-------|--------|\n24\t| TEE (G-theory) | Mesting 2026 | REML + D-study, facets: item/prompt/judge/temp |\n25\t| Total Variance | Brittlebench 2026 | V_data + V_brittleness via ANOVA |\n26\t| Intent Decomp | Kunievsky 2025 | intent/articulation/model uncertainty |\n27\t| Background Temp | Messina 2026 | Reference calibration→single T_bg scalar |\n28\t\n29\tG-theory adaptation is the most rigorous approach (Mesting). 4/10 papers use variance decomposition. **High confidence.**\n30\t\n31\t## Benchmarks & Models\n32\t\n33\tTop benchmarks: MMLU (3), GPQA (3), MATH (3), ARC (2), TruthfulQA (2). Models: Llama/Qwen/GPT families (5 papers each); 7B-8B open-weight as standard testbed. 10-100 runs per config is norm. **High confidence.**\n34\t\n35\t## Mitigations\n36\t\n37\t- LayerCast (BF16 store+FP32 compute): <3.4% divergence, 34% memory savings (Yuan). **Medium.**\n38\t- Batch-invariant kernels: determinism at 1.6-2.1× latency (ThinkingMachines). **Medium.**\n39\t- TEE-guided design: halves RMSE; 3 judges→38% SE reduction (Mesting). **Medium.**\n40\t\n41\t## Critical Gap — Our Niche\n42\t\n43\t**No paper unifies infrastructure (precision/batch/hardware) with methodological (prompt/judge/temp) variance in one G-theory framework.** Mesting applies G-theory to methodological facets only; Yuan/ThinkingMachines characterize infra variance without G-theory. Additional gaps: no cross-lingual analysis; training-time seed variance disconnected from inference-time; no standardized variance reporting protocol. **Support: 10/10 (absence), high confidence.**\n44\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5e85bec-84c8-44f3-af46-6eebdfb5f517","uuid":"590f2928-ac77-4b7d-a593-922330b87105","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:53:29.644Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/skills/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/distilled-research.skill.md","content":"---\nname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Research Patterns\ndescription: Experimental consensus on variance sources, decomposition methods, and gaps in LLM evaluation reproducibility (10 papers, 2024-2026)\ntype: research\nsource_papers: 10\n---\n\n## Variance Source Taxonomy\n\n**Infrastructure variance** (4/10; high confidence):\n- Numerical precision: BF16→9% accuracy std; FP32→~0% (Yuan 2025). Reasoning models most affected.\n- Batch-size dependence: 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-invariance failure in RMSNorm/GEMM/attention (ThinkingMachines 2025).\n- Background randomness at T=0: GPT~0.075, Gemini~0.065, Claude~0 effective temperature (Messina TMLR 2026).\n\n**Methodological variance** (6/10; high confidence):\n- Judge choice dominates safety eval (43.8% variance); prompt~50% in open models, ~25% commercial (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026).\n- Much apparent prompt sensitivity is evaluation artifact — LLM-judge raises rank correlation 0.30→0.95 (Hua EMNLP 2025).\n- Standard CIs omit 40-60% uncertainty; naive 95% CIs→79% coverage at scale (Mesting 2026).\n\n## Decomposition Frameworks\n\n| Framework | Paper | Method |\n|-----------|-------|--------|\n| TEE (G-theory) | Mesting 2026 | REML + D-study, facets: item/prompt/judge/temp |\n| Total Variance | Brittlebench 2026 | V_data + V_brittleness via ANOVA |\n| Intent Decomp | Kunievsky 2025 | intent/articulation/model uncertainty |\n| Background Temp | Messina 2026 | Reference calibration→single T_bg scalar |\n\nG-theory adaptation is the most rigorous approach (Mesting). 4/10 papers use variance decomposition. **High confidence.**\n\n## Benchmarks & Models\n\nTop benchmarks: MMLU (3), GPQA (3), MATH (3), ARC (2), TruthfulQA (2). Models: Llama/Qwen/GPT families (5 papers each); 7B-8B open-weight as standard testbed. 10-100 runs per config is norm. **High confidence.**\n\n## Mitigations\n\n- LayerCast (BF16 store+FP32 compute): <3.4% divergence, 34% memory savings (Yuan). **Medium.**\n- Batch-invariant kernels: determinism at 1.6-2.1× latency (ThinkingMachines). **Medium.**\n- TEE-guided design: halves RMSE; 3 judges→38% SE reduction (Mesting). **Medium.**\n\n## Critical Gap — Our Niche\n\n**No paper unifies infrastructure (precision/batch/hardware) with methodological (prompt/judge/temp) variance in one G-theory framework.** Mesting applies G-theory to methodological facets only; Yuan/ThinkingMachines characterize infra variance without G-theory. Additional gaps: no cross-lingual analysis; training-time seed variance disconnected from inference-time; no standardized variance reporting protocol. **Support: 10/10 (absence), high confidence.**\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}}}
