{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjSxdHHwTy6s5fp9T7LTfQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\n3\tdescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\n4\ttype: writing\n5\tsource_papers: 8\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Story Types\n9\t\n10\t- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n11\t- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n12\t- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n13\t- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n14\t\n15\t## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n16\t\n17\t\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n18\t\n19\t## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n20\t\n21\t(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n22\t\n23\t## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n24\t\n25\t(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n26\t\n27\t## Figure Conventions\n28\t\n29\t**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n30\t\n31\t**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n32\t\n33\t**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n34\t\n35\t## Table Conventions\n36\t\n37\t**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n38\t\n39\t**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n40\t\n41\t**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n42\t\n43\t**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n44\t\n45\t## Our Paper Blueprint\n46\t\n47\tOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5e85bec-84c8-44f3-af46-6eebdfb5f517","uuid":"64835611-073e-4847-b212-d1dfde15a726","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:53:37.152Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/skills/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/distilled-writing.skill.md","content":"---\nname: LLM Evaluation Variance — Writing Patterns\ndescription: Narrative strategies, figure/table conventions from LLM evaluation variance papers (8 deep-read, 2024-2026)\ntype: writing\nsource_papers: 8\n---\n\n## Story Types\n\n- **Breaking limitations** (3/8): Brittlebench, Yuan, ThinkingMachines. \"Everyone assumed X, we show it fails.\" High.\n- **Simplification** (2/8): Hua, GeneralizIT. \"Complex problem, simpler real answer.\" Medium.\n- **Unifying framework** (2/8): Kunievsky, Messina. \"Scattered observations, one formalism.\" Medium.\n- **Cross-domain transfer** (1/8): Mesting (psychometrics→ML). Our paper fits this type — study Mesting's structure. Low but critical.\n\n## Hook: Counter-Intuitive Claim Dominates (4/8, high)\n\n\"Greedy≠deterministic\" (Yuan), \"80/1000 unique at T=0\" (ThinkingMachines), \"sensitivity is artifact\" (Hua), \"95% CIs→79% coverage\" (Mesting). **Pattern**: open with a concrete number violating expectations. Avoid vague \"X is important.\"\n\n## Tension: \"Everyone's Doing It Wrong\" (4/8, high)\n\n(1) Cite widespread practice, (2) quantified evidence it fails, (3) identify overlooked factor. ThinkingMachines adds a twist: debunk the accepted explanation before presenting the correct one.\n\n## Intro: 4 Paragraphs + 3 Contributions (mode)\n\n(1) Context, (2) existing approaches + limitation, (3) gap/surprise, (4) contributions. 3 contributions standard: framework + empirical finding + practical tool. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n## Figure Conventions\n\n**Figure 1**: problem_illustration (4/8) — show problem exists before solution. Concrete examples with before/after or divergence visualization. **High.**\n\n**Result figures**: bar charts primary (5/8); stacked bars for variance decomposition (2/8, Mesting/Brittlebench — expected idiom for our paper); line plots for trends (3/8); heatmaps for divergence (2/8). **High.**\n\n**Figure count**: 4-6 total. **Flow**: problem illustration → method diagram → variance decomposition → D-study projections. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Table Conventions\n\n**Main table**: rows=models/configs, cols=benchmarks×metrics, grouped by category (commercial/open or reasoning/non-reasoning). Bold best, 4-8 baselines. **Support: 5/8, high.**\n\n**Ablation**: full_numbers (5/8) over checkmark_grid (2/8). Checkmark only for component-presence ablation. **High.**\n\n**Reporting**: std across configs/runs standard; only Mesting reports full REML components. **High.**\n\n**Figure vs table split**: figures for decomposition visuals/trends/qualitative; tables for exact numbers/comparisons. Appendix for per-config breakdowns and validation. **Support: 6/8, high.**\n\n## Our Paper Blueprint\n\nOpen with shocking quantified finding about unreported variance → \"everyone's CIs are wrong\" tension → G-theory as borrowed lens → 2-3 domain demonstrations → stacked-bar variance decomposition as signature visual → D-study projections as practical value.\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y4xsZHmBFSCqCtExmx2Siw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me organize my review process.\n\nThe paper is: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks and Models\"\n\nIt's a 14-page paper (including references and appendix) applying G-theory to LLM evaluation variance decomposition across 4 models and 4 benchmarks.\n\nThe paper doesn't specify a target venue. The review_context.yaml mentions AAAI as the target. Let me check if AAAI has specific reviewer guidelines. Since no venue is specified in the paper itself, and the instructions say to use ACL ARR 1-5 scale if I can't confirm venue. \n\nI'll look up AAAI's reviewer guidelines to be thorough, then fall back to the ACL ARR scale if needed. Once I have the framework in place, I'll dispatch two reviewers in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—before bringing them together for the roundtable discussion.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18935,"cache_read_input_tokens":42192,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18935},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5e85bec-84c8-44f3-af46-6eebdfb5f517","uuid":"ea33731f-a9f8-4c95-be82-f981e20a110a"}
