{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xay766RxMufRReeFVxcA7f","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my search, I found partial information about the AAAI review system, but the official numerical scale and complete scoring rubric are not fully documented in publicly available sources. Here's what I found:\n\n## AAAI 2026 Reviewer Scoring — What I Found\n\n**Evaluation Criteria (Confirmed):**\nReviewers assess papers on:\n- Problem clarity and significance\n- Technical soundness\n- Novelty and contribution\n- Empirical validation and reproducibility\n- Related work context\n- Evaluation quality (appropriate baselines, metrics, benchmarks)\n- Clarity of exposition\n\n**Numerical Scale:**\nThe search results reference scores in the **5-10 range** but do not provide an official published breakdown of what each score means. References indicate:\n- Scores typically range around 5.0–5.8 for most papers at AAAI 2026\n- Scores below 4.5 are relatively rare\n- Scores above 6 are considered standout submissions\n\n**Key Limitation:**\nThe official AAAI 2026 Reviewer Instructions page exists but does not publicly specify the exact numerical scale definitions (e.g., \"9 = Strong Accept, 8 = Accept, etc.\"). This detail appears to be available only within the submission/review system itself, not in public documentation.\n\n**What I Recommend:**\nCheck the AAAI submission portal directly or contact the conference program committee — the actual review form with score definitions (weak reject, borderline, accept, strong accept) is part of the reviewer interface but not published as standalone documentation online.\n\nSources:\n- [Instructions for AAAI-26 Reviewers](https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/instructions-for-aaai-26-reviewers/)\n- [AAAI-26 Review Process Update](https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/review-process-update/)\n- [AI-Assisted Peer Review at Scale: The AAAI-26 AI Review Pilot](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.13940)\n- [Overview of the AI Review System](https://aaai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/FAQ-for-the-AI-Assisted-Peer-Review-Process-Pilot-Program.pdf)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5e85bec-84c8-44f3-af46-6eebdfb5f517","uuid":"62c87ccc-5e82-40e7-94e9-dcd74edcf18e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:55:17.414Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Search the web for \"AAAI 2026 reviewer guidelines scoring criteria\" or \"AAAI conference paper review form rating scale\". I need to know the official AAAI paper review scoring scale — what numerical scale they use (e.g., 1-10), what each score means, and what criteria reviewers are asked to evaluate (soundness, significance, novelty, clarity, etc.). Report the scale and criteria concisely.","agentId":"a6a3d9411bb6cf0cf","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my search, I found partial information about the AAAI review system, but the official numerical scale and complete scoring rubric are not fully documented in publicly available sources. Here's what I found:\n\n## AAAI 2026 Reviewer Scoring — What I Found\n\n**Evaluation Criteria (Confirmed):**\nReviewers assess papers on:\n- Problem clarity and significance\n- Technical soundness\n- Novelty and contribution\n- Empirical validation and reproducibility\n- Related work context\n- Evaluation quality (appropriate baselines, metrics, benchmarks)\n- Clarity of exposition\n\n**Numerical Scale:**\nThe search results reference scores in the **5-10 range** but do not provide an official published breakdown of what each score means. References indicate:\n- Scores typically range around 5.0–5.8 for most papers at AAAI 2026\n- Scores below 4.5 are relatively rare\n- Scores above 6 are considered standout submissions\n\n**Key Limitation:**\nThe official AAAI 2026 Reviewer Instructions page exists but does not publicly specify the exact numerical scale definitions (e.g., \"9 = Strong Accept, 8 = Accept, etc.\"). This detail appears to be available only within the submission/review system itself, not in public documentation.\n\n**What I Recommend:**\nCheck the AAAI submission portal directly or contact the conference program committee — the actual review form with score definitions (weak reject, borderline, accept, strong accept) is part of the reviewer interface but not published as standalone documentation online.\n\nSources:\n- [Instructions for AAAI-26 Reviewers](https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/instructions-for-aaai-26-reviewers/)\n- [AAAI-26 Review Process Update](https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/review-process-update/)\n- [AI-Assisted Peer Review at Scale: The AAAI-26 AI Review Pilot](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.13940)\n- [Overview of the AI Review System](https://aaai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/FAQ-for-the-AI-Assisted-Peer-Review-Process-Pilot-Program.pdf)"}],"totalDurationMs":83668,"totalTokens":30688,"totalToolUseCount":13,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1462,"cache_read_input_tokens":28678,"output_tokens":544,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1462},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":544,"cache_read_input_tokens":28678,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1462,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1462,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019F1inAXfSUFM89UenLszjq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"AAAI's exact scale isn't publicly documented. The review_context.yaml mentions AAAI as target with borderline accept (5.5-6.0/10). Since I can't confirm the exact AAAI scale, I'll use ACL ARR 1-5 scale as instructed. Let me now dispatch the two reviewers in parallel.\n\nLet me prepare the full paper summary for the reviewers, since they need comprehensive context.\n\nKey paper details:\n- Title: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks and Models\"\n\n\n- Applies G-theory to decompose variance in LLM evaluation across 4 open-weight models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT, Mistral-7B-v0.3, Qwen3-8B) and 4 benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K) using 2.2M+ evaluation records\n- Examines variance across multiple facets including precision, prompt, seed, temperature, and ordering, with 4 main contributions: identifying gaps between IRT and G-theory approaches, analyzing model×item interactions, deriving benchmark-conditional variance estimates, and providing a D-study toolkit\n- Some experiments remain incomplete\n\n: multi-hardware evaluation, 14B model variants, LayerCast integration, and MATH benchmark testing, plus I'm still working through the G-study fine-tuning on 2 of 4 tasks with RoBERTa\n- Now I need to load the review report schema and run the parallel dispatches for R1 and R2","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1188,"cache_read_input_tokens":61127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1188},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5e85bec-84c8-44f3-af