{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019F1inAXfSUFM89UenLszjq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MyHo6g5K8n5pwN7ZWMvqq3","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"claude","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an academic paper review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Scoring Scale: ACL ARR 1-5\n- 5 = Top 2-5%: groundbreaking\n- 4 = Top 10-20%: solid, impactful\n- 3 = Top 30-50%: borderline, has merits but also significant weaknesses\n- 2 = Below average: significant issues\n- 1 = Seriously flawed\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks and Models\"\n\n**Core idea**: Apply Generalizability Theory (G-theory) from psychometrics to decompose LLM evaluation variance across infrastructure (numerical precision) and methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) facets. Study 4 open-weight models (~7-9B) on 4 benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K), totaling 2.2M+ records.\n\n**4 Contributions**:\n1. IRT vs G-theory gap: G-theory requires 3.6-7.0× more items than IRT for G≥0.80, because IRT ignores prompt×item, seed×item, ordering×item dimensions\n2. Model×item interaction: 16.4% of variance [95% CI: 11.2-21.8%] when model is crossed facet, collapsing cross-model G from 0.598 to 0.152\n3. Benchmark-conditional variance structure: item dominance ranges from 59.7% (MMLU) to 41.8% (ARC), gradient approximately but not strictly monotonic\n4. Open-source D-study toolkit for benchmark-specific reproducibility budgets\n\n**Experiments completed**:\n- exp-001: 4-model full-scale MMLU G-study (890,400 records). Single-model decomposition + cross-model with model as random facet\n- exp-001b: Item resampling stability (3 independent 200-item samples, Llama only, 10,800 records/sample)\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark replication on GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag (1,382,400 records across 3 benchmarks)\n- exp-004: Comprehensive analysis — bootstrap CIs, IRT comparison, D-study, methodological defenses (Bernoulli correlation, fixed vs random, logprob scoring, etc.)\n- exp-006: Partial fine-tuning G-study — RoBERTa on SST-2 and MNLI only (288 runs, 16.7% of planned)\n\n**Experiments NOT completed**:\n- exp-003: Multi-hardware G-study (no second GPU → hardware facet = only numerical precision on single RTX PRO 6000)\n- exp-005: 14B model extension (hardware constraints)\n- exp-007: LayerCast precision mitigation (P2 priority, not done)\n- exp-008: MATH benchmark extension (P2 priority, not done)\n\n**Key methodological details**:\n- vLLM 0.9.2 with enforce_eager mode (eliminates float32 computation engine confound)\n- Generation-based evaluation following Hua et al. (2025) to avoid log-likelihood scoring artifacts\n- Fully crossed design for MMLU: 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items\n- Reduced design for other benchmarks: 2 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items\n- Bootstrap CIs: 1000 resamples stratified by item\n- Henderson expected mean squares for variance estimation\n- G-coefficient formula: G = σ²_item / (σ²_item + Σ_f σ²_{f×item}/n_f + σ²_residual/Π_f n_f)\n\n**Key results by table**:\n- Table 1: Per-model single-facet decomposition — item_id dominates (mean 59.7%), G ranges 0.571-0.628\n- Table 2: 4-model crossed — item_id 41.2%, model×item 16.4%, model 6.8%\n- Table 3: FP32 degrades accuracy 5-11pp below BF16 due to enforce_eager mode\n- Table 4: D-study projections — even 20 models × 1000 items → G=0.612 (below 0.80)\n- Table 5: Item resampling — item_id CV=0.13 across 3 samples\n- Table 7: Cross-benchmark — item_id from 59.7% (MMLU) to 41.8% (ARC)\n- Table 8: Cross-benchmark summary with G-coefficients\n- Table 10: Bootstrap CIs — model×item CI width 10.6pp\n- Table 11: IRT vs G-theory — 3.6-7.0× ratio\n- Table 12: Methodological defenses (6 analyses)\n- Table 13: D-study budget calculator\n- Table 14: Inference vs fine-tuning variance hierarchy comparison\n\n**Precision anomaly**: FP32 performs WORSE than BF16/FP16. The paper explains this as a vLLM enforce_eager artifact — FP32 uses eager-mode computation path while BF16/FP16 use optimized CUDA graphs, resulting in different numerical trajectories. This is not a \"FP32 is less precise\" finding but rather an implementation-dependent confound.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Soundness of methodology** (G-theory application, experimental design, statistical analysis)\n2. **Experimental coverage and completeness** (what's done vs planned, impact of missing experiments)\n3. **Statistical rigor** (bootstrap CIs, power analysis, robustness checks)\n4. **Reproducibility and transparency** (enough detail to replicate?)\n5. **Weaknesses in experimental design**\n\nFor each dimension, give specific examples from the paper. Note both strengths and weaknesses. Be critical but fair.\n\nProvide scores (ACL ARR 1-5) for:\n- Soundness\n- Substance (experimental breadth/depth)\n- Overall recommendation\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with clear sections. Be specific — cite table numbers, section numbers, and exact figures from the paper."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1188,"cache_read_input_tokens":61127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1188},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5e85bec-84c8-44f3-af46-6eebdfb5f517","uuid":"7e064b20-fed9-4f23-abec-2a5604bd8791"}
