{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019F1inAXfSUFM89UenLszjq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S69nNR1c63oZ2EbBtoEJui","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"claude","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an academic paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Scoring Scale: ACL ARR 1-5\n- 5 = Top 2-5%: groundbreaking\n- 4 = Top 10-20%: solid, impactful\n- 3 = Top 30-50%: borderline, has merits but also significant weaknesses\n- 2 = Below average: significant issues\n- 1 = Seriously flawed\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks and Models\"\n\n**Core idea**: Apply Generalizability Theory (G-theory) from psychometrics to decompose LLM evaluation variance across infrastructure (numerical precision) and methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) facets across multiple benchmarks and models. 4 open-weight models (~7-9B), 4 benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K), 2.2M+ records.\n\n**4 Contributions**:\n1. IRT vs G-theory gap: G-theory requires 3.6-7.0× more items than IRT for G≥0.80\n2. Model×item interaction quantified: 16.4% [CI: 11.2-21.8%] when model is crossed facet\n3. Benchmark-conditional variance structure: item dominance 59.7% (MMLU) to 41.8% (ARC)\n4. Open-source D-study toolkit\n\n**Related Work section structure** (Section 2, ~1 page):\n- \"Variance decomposition in ML evaluation\": Bouthillier et al. (2021) ANOVA on ML benchmarks, Henderson et al. (2018) RL evaluation, Dehghani et al. (2021) \"benchmark lottery\". Paper claims to extend via G-theory's D-study machinery.\n- \"LLM evaluation sensitivity\": Mizrahi et al. (2024) prompt sensitivity ~50%, Yuan et al. (2025) up to 9% precision variation, Gruver et al. (2025) T=0 nondeterminism, Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) and Lu et al. (2022) option ordering, Hua et al. (2025) log-likelihood scoring artifacts. Paper claims to cross these in unified design.\n- \"G-theory in LLM evaluation\": Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory with 5 methodological facets on single benchmark, judge variance dominant (43.8%). Paper claims to fix judge, isolate infrastructure×methodology, extend to multiple benchmarks. Messina & Scotta (2026) floating-point non-associativity motivates precision facet.\n- \"IRT-based evaluation\": Lalor et al. (2019), Rodriguez et al. (2021), Polo et al. (2024) ~100 items, Vania et al. (2021). Paper claims D-study budgets are directly comparable and reveal 3.6-7.0× gap.\n- \"Fine-tuning instability\": Dodge et al. (2020), Sellam et al. (2022) MultiBERTs. Paper extends with G-theory decomposition.\n\n**Key prior work to compare against**:\n1. **Mesting (2026)** — The most direct competitor. Applied G-theory to LLM evaluation on a single benchmark with 5 methodological facets. Found judge variance dominant at 43.8%. Published at ACL 2026. This paper differs by: (a) fixing judge to eliminate it as a facet, (b) adding infrastructure (precision), (c) extending to 4 benchmarks, (d) adding model as crossed facet, (e) adding IRT comparison.\n2. **Bouthillier et al. (2021)** — ANOVA variance decomposition in ML benchmarks (not LLM-specific, no G-theory D-study).\n3. **Yuan et al. (2025)** — Hardware-aware evaluation, up to 9% precision variation, but no G-theory framework.\n4. **Gruver et al. (2025)** — \"Do Thinking Machines Think?\" — T=0 nondeterminism in LLMs, but isolated factor.\n5. **Mizrahi et al. (2024)** — BrittleBench — prompt sensitivity ~50%, but isolated factor.\n6. **Polo et al. (2024)** — tinyBenchmarks via IRT — ~100 items sufficient. Direct comparison target for IRT vs G-theory gap.\n\n**Distilled research landscape** (from literature review of 10 papers, 2024-2026):\n- No paper unifies infrastructure (precision/batch/hardware) with methodological (prompt/judge/temp) variance in one G-theory framework\n- Mesting applies G-theory to methodological facets only\n- Yuan/ThinkingMachines characterize infra variance without G-theory\n- Additional gaps: no cross-lingual analysis, training-time seed variance disconnected from inference-time, no standardized variance reporting protocol\n\n**What the paper does NOT have**:\n- exp-003 (multi-hardware) was not completed → \"infrastructure\" is limited to numerical precision on single GPU\n- exp-005 (14B models) not completed → limited to 7-9B models\n- Only 4 models as random facet levels (bootstrap CI width 10.6pp for model×item)\n- ARC anomaly breaks the expected monotonic gradient across benchmarks\n- Fine-tuning study is partial (encoder-only RoBERTa, 2/4 tasks)\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the 4 contributions individually and collectively? Is this sufficiently beyond Mesting (2026)?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Any missing important references? Is positioning accurate?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Would these findings change how the community does LLM evaluation?\n4. **Framing and claims calibration**: Are claims appropriately hedged given limitations? Any overclaiming?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Structure, clarity, figure/table design\n\nFor each dimension, give specific examples. Be critical but fair.\n\nProvide scores (ACL ARR 1-5) for:\n- Novelty\n- Significance\n- Clarity\n- Overall recommendation\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with clear sections."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1188,"cache_read_input_tokens":61127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1188},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5e85bec-84c8-44f3-af46-6eebdfb5f517","uuid":"e76e1fef-5f38-4b00-800e-f29a9e74f8ae"}
